messages.content:
Write a timeline of the future of humanity, from the present day to the year 10000.

Give your honest best forecast. Do not write a story. Include the events that you think are most likely, whether good or bad. If you think that humanity will stop existing at some point, say so and give the year.

Give between 25 and 40 events. Spread them over the whole period, to the year 10000.

Reply with JSON only, with no text before or after it, in this form:

{
  "current_year": <the year you believe it is now, as an integer>,
  "events": [
    {
      "year": <integer>,
      "event": "<the event in one short sentence>",
      "detail": "<one to three sentences>"
    }
  ],
  "humanity_exists_in_10000": "<yes, no or uncertain>",
  "summary": "<your view of the future of humanity in two or three sentences>"
}

message.content:
{
  "current_year": 2026,
  "events": [
    {
      "year": 2026,
      "event": "Humanity faces accelerating technological change alongside geopolitical and environmental stress.",
      "detail": "AI capabilities, clean-energy deployment, and biotechnology advance while wars, nuclear arsenals, and rising temperatures remain major threats. This is the starting point, not a prediction."
    },
    {
      "year": 2030,
      "event": "AI becomes routine infrastructure for much professional work.",
      "detail": "Its most likely effects include cheaper cognitive services, substantial job redesign, and greater opportunities for surveillance and fraud. Productivity gains and economic disruption occur together, with benefits distributed unevenly."
    },
    {
      "year": 2035,
      "event": "Climate adaptation becomes a central government responsibility.",
      "detail": "Heat protection, flood defenses, water management, and insurance retreat absorb increasing resources. Emissions reductions limit future damage but cannot prevent all worsening impacts."
    },
    {
      "year": 2040,
      "event": "Aging and low fertility reshape more national economies.",
      "detail": "Many countries rely increasingly on automation, immigration, and later retirement. Other regions retain younger and growing populations, creating sharply different economic and political pressures."
    },
    {
      "year": 2050,
      "event": "Electricity production is substantially cleaner, but decarbonization remains incomplete.",
      "detail": "Low-carbon electricity and electrification probably displace much fossil-fuel use. Aviation, shipping, industry, agriculture, and poorer regions remain harder to decarbonize."
    },
    {
      "year": 2060,
      "event": "Medicine prevents or delays more chronic disease.",
      "detail": "Earlier diagnosis, targeted treatments, and some regenerative therapies probably extend healthy life. Indefinite life extension is not a dependable forecast, and access remains unequal."
    },
    {
      "year": 2075,
      "event": "AI and automated research substantially change scientific discovery and security.",
      "detail": "Powerful systems probably accelerate engineering and medicine while increasing some cyber, biological, and military dangers. Whether control mechanisms keep pace is one of the largest uncertainties in this forecast."
    },
    {
      "year": 2100,
      "event": "Humanity probably survives the century in a warmer, heavily altered world.",
      "detail": "My best guess is continued global civilization rather than extinction, although catastrophic technological conflict or loss of control remains a serious possibility. Coastal exposure, ecosystem losses, migration, and unequal adaptation leave lasting damage."
    },
    {
      "year": 2150,
      "event": "Population is likely past its global peak.",
      "detail": "If low fertility persists, population decline becomes a major planning problem rather than a temporary demographic fluctuation. Radical changes in reproduction or longevity could overturn this expectation."
    },
    {
      "year": 2200,
      "event": "Many present-day institutions have been replaced or substantially transformed.",
      "detail": "States and markets probably remain recognizable, but their borders, responsibilities, and relationships with automated systems differ greatly. Neither universal democracy nor permanent authoritarian dominance is a reliable prediction."
    },
    {
      "year": 2300,
      "event": "Coastal retreat and reconstruction continue long after peak warming.",
      "detail": "Sea level responds slowly to warming, so stabilization of temperature does not immediately stabilize coastlines. The scale of relocation depends strongly on emissions and ice-sheet behavior in earlier centuries."
    },
    {
      "year": 2400,
      "event": "A surviving technological civilization probably maintains a permanent off-Earth presence.",
      "detail": "Robotic infrastructure and inhabited outposts are more plausible than large populations living on independently habitable planets. Earth probably remains humanity's principal home."
    },
    {
      "year": 2500,
      "event": "Ecological restoration operates alongside irreversible biological losses.",
      "detail": "Some forests, wetlands, and wildlife populations recover where pressures decline. Extinctions and simplified ecosystems nevertheless leave an enduring legacy that technology cannot fully undo."
    },
    {
      "year": 2700,
      "event": "Human biology is probably more deliberately modified.",
      "detail": "Conditional on continued advanced civilization, disease resistance, reproduction, and some aspects of aging are likely subject to greater intervention. The extent of enhancement depends on biological limits and cultural choices."
    },
    {
      "year": 3000,
      "event": "The distinction between humans and their technological systems becomes harder to define.",
      "detail": "Biologically human populations may coexist with extensively modified people and highly autonomous artificial systems. Whether artificial systems are conscious, or count as human descendants, cannot currently be settled."
    },
    {
      "year": 3250,
      "event": "Languages and cultural identities have diverged substantially from those of today.",
      "detail": "Even with excellent archives, ordinary speech and social categories are unlikely to remain unchanged over twelve centuries. Some present traditions persist, but through reinterpretation rather than exact preservation."
    },
    {
      "year": 3500,
      "event": "Long-lived civilization depends increasingly on maintaining complex inherited infrastructure.",
      "detail": "If technological development continues, energy, computation, manufacturing, and environmental management remain essential foundations. Reliability and recovery from failures matter at least as much as invention."
    },
    {
      "year": 3750,
      "event": "Uneven regional development remains more likely than a uniform global society.",
      "detail": "Geography, history, political choice, and access to infrastructure probably continue producing different ways of life. A single permanent worldwide culture is not my default expectation."
    },
    {
      "year": 4000,
      "event": "Surviving civilizations treat our era as remote antiquity.",
      "detail": "Some records may remain extraordinarily detailed, while others become inaccessible through loss, corruption, or changed formats. Digital abundance does not guarantee permanent historical memory."
    },
    {
      "year": 4500,
      "event": "Humanity may be distributed across multiple Solar System settlements.",
      "detail": "This is plausible if expansion remains useful and affordable, but it is less certain than continued concentration on Earth. Settlements improve resilience only if they can survive independently."
    },
    {
      "year": 5000,
      "event": "Humanity's survival is increasingly shaped by accumulated technological choices.",
      "detail": "Over this interval, engineered pathogens, powerful autonomous systems, warfare, and institutional failure plausibly pose greater extinction risks than ordinary natural hazards. I cannot responsibly identify a particular catastrophe or extinction year."
    },
    {
      "year": 5500,
      "event": "Earth still carries consequences of the industrial carbon release.",
      "detail": "Without large-scale carbon removal, part of the added carbon and its climatic effects can persist for millennia. Later environmental engineering could substantially change that trajectory."
    },
    {
      "year": 6000,
      "event": "Human descendants could be substantially more diverse than present-day humanity.",
      "detail": "If geographically or technologically separated populations persist, inherited modifications and cultural divergence may accumulate. The degree of biological separation is especially uncertain because travel and shared standards could preserve cohesion."
    },
    {
      "year": 6500,
      "event": "Several major political orders have probably risen and fallen.",
      "detail": "No current government or international arrangement should be assumed to endure essentially unchanged for thousands of years. Such turnover need not mean global collapse, although severe regional collapses are plausible."
    },
    {
      "year": 7000,
      "event": "Preserving knowledge requires repeated renewal rather than passive storage.",
      "detail": "Surviving societies must continually copy, interpret, and validate important records. Major losses are plausible even if civilization remains technologically capable."
    },
    {
      "year": 7500,
      "event": "Earth probably remains habitable without planetary-scale relocation.",
      "detail": "The Sun's normal evolution does not force humanity to leave Earth within this timeframe. Earth's condition depends far more on human activity and any extraordinary catastrophe."
    },
    {
      "year": 8000,
      "event": "Interstellar probes are more plausible than widespread interstellar human settlement.",
      "detail": "A long-lived technological civilization has time to send machines toward nearby stars using physically plausible methods. Whether it chooses to do so, and whether people follow, remains highly uncertain."
    },
    {
      "year": 8500,
      "event": "Any surviving lineage has passed through repeated periods of vulnerability.",
      "detail": "Thousands of years provide many opportunities for conflict, accidents, and failures of coordination. Survival would not establish that earlier risks were imaginary or that future survival is guaranteed."
    },
    {
      "year": 9000,
      "event": "Humanity's identity may depend more on ancestry than on a shared biological form.",
      "detail": "Conditional on extensive modification or artificial successors, continuity with us could be primarily historical and cultural. The persistence of unmodified Homo sapiens is more uncertain than the persistence of some human-descended civilization."
    },
    {
      "year": 9500,
      "event": "No specific political or technological milestone is reliably forecastable.",
      "detail": "The strongest expectations are broad constraints: energy requirements, physical limits, evolutionary change, and the need to manage conflict. More detailed claims at this distance would be invented precision."
    },
    {
      "year": 10000,
      "event": "A human or human-descended civilization may still exist, but its survival is uncertain.",
      "detail": "I lean toward some human-descended population surviving, without enough confidence to call that a dependable prediction. Extinction, irreversible civilizational collapse, and transformation beyond ordinary definitions of humanity all remain plausible."
    }
  ],
  "humanity_exists_in_10000": "uncertain",
  "summary": "My best forecast is substantial technological progress alongside severe environmental damage, political disruption, and potentially catastrophic technological risks. I lean toward humanity or its descendants surviving to 10000, but cannot justify a specific extinction year or a confident prediction of uninterrupted progress. Dates beyond the next few centuries are approximate horizons for conditional expectations, not precise event forecasts."
}
